#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:1
msgid ""
"<div class=\"warning\">\n"
"WARNING: Current Windows support is experimental.\n"
"</div>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:5
msgid "Source Installation on Windows 10 or 11"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:7
msgid ""
"OGRE2 rendering capabilities are supported in Windows, and Gazebo GUI works "
"in a limited fashion. These functionalities correspond to the currently "
"building packages `gz-rendering` and `gz-sim`, respectively. The packages "
"will build, without any failures when using their functionalities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:12
msgid ""
"**NOTE** You should be able to use `ogre` as a rendering engine instead of "
"the default `ogre2`. Just append `--render-engine ogre` to the command line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:16
msgid "Install dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:18
msgid ""
"If you do not have the conda package manager installed in your system, "
"install a conda distribution. As Gazebo uses all dependencies from the conda-"
"forge channel, we suggest to install miniforge following [the official "
"miniforge installation docs](https://github.com/conda-forge/"
"miniforge#windows) by executing the following commands in a \"Command "
"Prompt\" terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:20
msgid ""
"curl.exe -L -O https://github.com/conda-forge/miniforge/releases/latest/"
"download/Miniforge3-Windows-x86_64.exe\n"
"start /wait \"\" Miniforge3-Windows-x86_64.exe /InstallationType=JustMe /"
"RegisterPython=0 /S /D=%UserProfile%\\Miniforge3\n"
"%UserProfile%\\Miniforge3\\condabin\\conda init\n"
"%UserProfile%\\Miniforge3\\condabin\\conda config --set auto_activate_base "
"false\n"
"del Miniforge3-Windows-x86_64.exe\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:27
msgid ""
"If you followed this step correctly, you should be able to access `conda` in "
"any new \"Command Prompt\" terminal you open. To verify that, check that if "
"you open a new terminal and execute `conda info`, the output begins with:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:29
msgid ""
"  active environment : None\n"
"         shell level : 0\n"
"  [..]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:35
msgid ""
"Install [Visual Studio 2019 or 2022](https://visualstudio.microsoft.com/"
"downloads/). The Community version is free for students, open-source "
"developers, and personal development. Check \"Desktop development with C++\" "
"in the Workloads tab, check \"MFC and ATL support\", and uncheck \"C++ Cmake "
"Tools.\" We will install cmake via Conda. All other checkboxes can be left "
"unchecked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:41
msgid ""
"Open a Visual Studio Command Prompt (search for \"x64 Native Tools Command "
"Prompt for VS\" in the Windows search field near the Windows button) or "
"Developer PowerShell for VS (search for \"developer powershell\"). "
"Optionally, right-click and pin to the task bar for quick access in the "
"future."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:46
msgid ""
"If you chose PowerShell, you need to do a few steps to be able to use Conda "
"and Gazebo in it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:47
msgid ""
"conda init powershell\n"
"# Restart the PowerShell\n"
"Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy Unrestricted -Scope CurrentUser\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:53
msgid "Create and activate a Conda environment:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:54
msgid ""
"conda create -n gz-ws\n"
"conda activate gz-ws\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:58
msgid ""
"Once you have activated an environment, a prefix like `(gz-ws)` will be "
"prepended to your prompt."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:61
msgid "You can use `conda info --envs` to see all of your environments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:63
msgid ""
"To speed up conda installations, also set the following to use libmamba "
"solver. Older conda installations may need to do [additional steps](https://"
"www.anaconda.com/blog/a-faster-conda-for-a-growing-community)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:65
msgid "conda config --set solver libmamba\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:68
msgid "To remove an environment, use `conda remove --all --name <env_name>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:70
msgid ""
"**NOTE** This way of Conda environment creation puts it into a default "
"folder. If you need      to install it elsewhere, use `--prefix <env_path>` "
"instead of `--name <env_name>`.      Environments in custom paths cannot be "
"referenced by names, so even `conda activate`      needs to be passed a path "
"(relative or absolute) instead of the name. If you refer      to a "
"subdirectory of the current directory, you have to prepend `.\\` so that "
"Conda      knows it is a path and not a name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:78
msgid "Install dependencies:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:79
msgid ""
"conda install cmake git vcstool curl pkg-config ^\n"
"colcon-common-extensions dartsim eigen freeimage gdal gts ^\n"
"glib dlfcn-win32 ffmpeg ruby tinyxml2 tinyxml ^\n"
"protobuf urdfdom zeromq cppzmq ogre=1.10 ogre-next jsoncpp ^\n"
"libzip qt pybind11 boost --channel conda-forge\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:86
msgid "This can take tens of minutes (or less when using libmamba solver)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:88
msgid ""
"Navigate to where you would like to build the library, create and enter your "
"workspace directory, create the `src` directory which will contain the "
"Gazebo source code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:90
msgid ""
"mkdir gz-ws\n"
"cd gz-ws\n"
"mkdir src\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:96
msgid "Then clone the repositories"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:97
msgid ""
"# Both CMD and PowerShell\n"
"vcs import --input https://raw.githubusercontent.com/gazebo-tooling/"
"gazebodistro/master/collection-ionic.yaml src\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:102
msgid "Building the Gazebo Libraries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:104
msgid ""
"Once the compiler and all the sources are in place it is time to compile "
"them. Start the procedure by navigating to your workspace and listing the "
"packages recognized by `colcon`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:108
msgid "colcon graph\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:112
msgid ""
"`colcon graph` should list the Gazebo libraries with an [interdependency "
"diagram](https://colcon.readthedocs.io/en/released/reference/verb/graph."
"html#example-output). If that is the case, then you are ready to build the "
"whole set of libraries:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:116
msgid ""
"colcon build --cmake-args -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF -DSKIP_SWIG=ON --merge-install "
"--packages-up-to gz-sim9 gz-tools2\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:119
msgid "Tests are turned off as they are not currently supported on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:121
msgid "To build a specific package with all its dependent packages:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:123
msgid "colcon build --merge-install --packages-up-to PACKAGE_NAME\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:127
msgid "To build a single package:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:129
msgid "colcon build --packages-select PACKAGE_NAME\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:133
msgid ""
"Visit [colcon documentation](https://colcon.readthedocs.io/en/released/#) to "
"view more `colcon` build and test options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:135
msgid "If there are no errors, all the binaries should be ready to use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:137
msgid "Using the workspace"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:139
msgid ""
"The workspace needs to be sourced every time a new terminal is used ( and "
"Conda environment activated before that)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:142
msgid ""
"The overall instructions for setting up a new terminal to use the built "
"workspace are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:145
msgid ""
"# CMD\n"
"conda activate gz-ws\n"
"call install\\setup.bat\n"
"\n"
"# PowerShell\n"
"conda activate gz-ws\n"
".\\install\\setup.ps1\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:155
msgid "You should now be able to launch gazebo:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:157
msgid ""
"# Launch server in one terminal \n"
"gz sim -s\n"
"\n"
"# In separate terminal, launch gui\n"
"gz sim -g\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:165
msgid ""
"This is the end of the source install instructions; head back to the "
"[Getting started](getstarted) page to start using Gazebo!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:168
msgid ""
"**NOTE** If your username contains spaces (which is quite common on "
"Windows), you will probably get errors  saying `Invalid partition name "
"[Computer:My User With Spaces]`. Fix this by changing `GZ_PARTITION`  to "
"something else:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:172
msgid "set GZ_PARTITION=test\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:175
msgid "Remember to set the same partition in all other consoles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:177
msgid "Gazebo GUI workaround"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:179
msgid ""
"Although running `gz sim` without arguments is not supported on Windows,  "
"the `gz sim -g` command is  supported, and you can use it to launch the "
"graphical interface on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:183
msgid ""
"This should allow you to run the GUI in a separate console, connecting to "
"the server running in another console."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:185
msgid "Uninstalling source-based install"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:187
msgid ""
"A source-based install can be \"uninstalled\" using several methods, "
"depending on the results you want:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:190
msgid ""
"If you installed your workspace with `colcon` as instructed above, "
"\"uninstalling\" could be just a matter of opening a new terminal and not "
"sourcing the workspace's `setup.bat`. This way, your environment will behave "
"as though Gazebo is not installed on your system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:195
msgid ""
"If, in addition to not wanting to use the libraries, you're also trying to "
"free up space, you can delete the entire workspace directory from within "
"your `conda` environment with:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:199
msgid "rm -rf <workspace_name>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:203
msgid ""
"If you want to keep the source code, you can remove the `install` / "
"`build` / `log` directories as desired, leaving the `src` directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:206
msgid ""
"Last, if you do not need the conda environment anymore, you can remove it "
"with"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:208
msgid ""
"conda deactivate\n"
"conda remove --all --name <env_name>\n"
"# or conda remove --all --prefix <path_to_env> if you installed to custom "
"path\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:214
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../install_windows_src.md:216
msgid "See [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md#windows)"
msgstr ""
